<Project>

  <!--
    Nullable-reference warnings are treated as build errors.

    Rationale: a single over-broad implicit conversion operator once produced 150 nullable
    warnings across the solution, and a genuine null-dereference defect in SmtpEmailSender
    sat hidden among them. Escalating these specific codes keeps that signal from being
    buried again.

    Scope is deliberately narrow — only the nullable-flow codes are escalated. Unrelated
    warnings (ASP0000, NU1510, CS0618, ...) still surface as warnings and never break a build.

    The Condition excludes Auth_DB.sqlproj, which is an SSDT project compiled by a separate
    .NET Framework toolchain that does not share these diagnostics.

    NuGet audit (known-vulnerable packages) is set explicitly even though these values are
    today's SDK defaults for net10.0: the default mode is tied to the target framework and has
    changed between SDK releases, and an environment variable named NuGetAudit can switch the
    audit off for a project that does not define the property. NuGetAuditLevel is the lowest
    severity REPORTED, not a failure threshold. Every restore, locally and in CI, reports
    advisories for direct and transitive packages as NU1901-NU1904 warnings.

    Advisories become errors only when AuditPipeline=true, which only the "Dependency audit"
    workflow (.github/workflows/dependency-audit.yml) passes. NU1902 (moderate) and above fail
    it; NU1900 (the vulnerability data could not be read) and NU1905 (a configured audit
    source has no vulnerability data) fail it closed. The build and the required "Backend
    build and test" job never pass AuditPipeline, so an advisory published tomorrow against a
    package nobody touched cannot block a merge; it turns the scheduled audit red instead.

    Under AuditPipeline the target AssertNuGetAuditRan (below) also proves the audit ran: the
    restore must report as many audited projects as restored ones. Without it, a package
    source that carries no vulnerability data (NuGet then stays silent), NuGetAudit=false set
    in one project, or a static graph restore (which reports no counts) would pass in silence.

    Tools/github/governance.test.mjs (guard G-S03h) keeps all of this so: no other file may
    set NuGetAudit*, hide NU190x, redirect NuGet's configuration or sources, import other
    files or redefine the target, and TreatWarningsAsErrors=true needs a WarningsNotAsErrors
    exception for NU1900-NU1905 under '$(AuditPipeline)' != 'true'.

    Suppression is the last resort and only for an accepted risk (Tools/github/README.md).
    Add it as an item in an ItemGroup below, with an XML comment on the line directly above it
    (G-S03h; the dashes are spaced here only because XML comments cannot nest), the expiry
    at most 90 days after the date:
      <!- - allow GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx YYYY-MM-DD until YYYY-MM-DD <reason> - ->
      <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx" />
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectExtension)' == '.csproj'">
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CS8600;CS8601;CS8602;CS8603;CS8604;CS8618;CS8619;CS8620;CS8625</WarningsAsErrors>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectExtension)' == '.csproj' And '$(AuditPipeline)' == 'true'">
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1900;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904;NU1905</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Runs after the restore of the project dotnet restore was given; the counts cover its
       whole graph. Empty or zero counts fail too: static graph restore reports none. -->
  <Target Name="AssertNuGetAuditRan" AfterTargets="Restore" Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectExtension)' == '.csproj' And '$(AuditPipeline)' == 'true'">
    <Error Condition="'$(RestoreProjectCount)' == '' Or '$(RestoreProjectCount)' == '0' Or '$(RestoreProjectsAuditedCount)' != '$(RestoreProjectCount)'"
           Text="NuGet audit did not run for every restored project: $(RestoreProjectsAuditedCount) audited of $(RestoreProjectCount) restored. Check NuGetAudit, the package and audit sources, and static graph restore (Tools/github/README.md)." />
  </Target>

</Project>
